<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email: </span><a href="mailto:"><span id="id3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">samuel.horvath@mbzuai.ac.ae</span></a>
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Alleviating catastrophic forgetting while enabling further learning is a primary challenge in continual learning (CL). Orthogonal-based training methods have gained attention for their efficiency and strong theoretical properties, and many existing approaches enforce orthogonality through gradient projection.
In this paper, we revisit orthogonality and exploit the fact that small singular values correspond to directions that are nearly orthogonal to the input space of previous tasks. Building on this principle, we introduce NESS (Null-space Estimated from Small Singular values), a CL method that applies orthogonality directly in the weight space rather than through gradient manipulation. Specifically, NESS constructs an approximate null space using the smallest singular values of each layer’s input representation and parameterizes task-specific updates via a compact low-rank adaptation (LoRA-style) formulation constrained to this subspace. The subspace basis is fixed to preserve the null-space constraint, and only a single trainable matrix is learned for each task. This design ensures that the resulting updates remain approximately in the null space of previous inputs while enabling adaptation to new tasks.
Our theoretical analysis and experiments on three benchmark datasets demonstrate competitive performance, low forgetting, and stable accuracy across tasks, highlighting the role of small singular values in continual learning.
The code is available at <a href="https://github.com/pacman-ctm/NESS" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href ltx_font_typewriter">https://github.com/pacman-ctm/NESS</a>.</p>
  
</div>
<div id="id4" class="ltx_logical-block ltx_align_bottom">
    <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:100%;height:0.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>
    <span class="ltx_rule ltx_align_center" style="width:100%;height:0.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Continual learning (CL) is a machine learning approach that trains a single model through a sequence of tasks with varying properties while maintaining performance on all previous tasks. In CL, <span id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">catastrophic forgetting</span> is the most significant challenge, in which the performance of learned tasks decreases drastically after continual training on new tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>. Addressing this phenomenon is the key to designing an effective CL algorithm.
As machine learning models are deployed in more complex, dynamic data and applications, the need for a stable model that reduces forgetting is emerging as a key characteristic for designing an efficient CL algorithm. Due to the need to adapt to new tasks, CL approaches must find a balance between <span id="S1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">plasticity</span> (the ability to adapt to new information) and <span id="S1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">stability</span> (the ability to retain past knowledge) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">There are many approaches to building an efficient CL model with a low forgetting rate, and Singular Value Decomposition (SVD)-based methods have been a popular choice for maintaining performance after training on new tasks. Early approaches to mitigating forgetting focused primarily on regularizing the weight changes <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>. More recent approaches, on the other hand, were based on storing episodic memories from previous data and adding constraints based on this information to the gradient direction, thus reducing the forgetting of old tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2602.21919v1/model.drawio.svg" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:452/217;" width="452" height="217"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>Overview of NESS. During continual training (left), each task updates the network sequentially. For every task <math id="S1.F1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> and layer <math id="S1.F1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math>, we collect the concatenated inputs from previous tasks and compute the SVD of the corresponding covariance matrix (right). The update is parameterized as a low-rank decomposition <math id="S1.F1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta W_{t}^{(l)}=U_{t}^{(l)}V_{t}^{(l)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta W_{t}^{(l)}=U_{t}^{(l)}V_{t}^{(l)}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S1.F1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{t}^{(l)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{t}^{(l)}</annotation></semantics></math> is a frozen orthogonal basis constructed from singular vectors associated with small singular values, and <math id="S1.F1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{t}^{(l)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{t}^{(l)}</annotation></semantics></math> is a trainable matrix initialized to zero. This structured update constrains learning to an approximate null subspace of previous inputs, limiting interference while enabling adaptation to the current task.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Using gradient projection is currently favored for mitigating catastrophic forgetting, with the pioneering work GPM proposed by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Saha et al. [8]</a></cite>, which utilized the null space of previously learned tasks for gradient projection, thus preventing interference with learned knowledge. They also proposed a <span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">backward transfer</span> metric to measure forgetting during continual training, which is widely used to evaluate later CL models.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">A large body of SVD-based continual learning methods relies on a common mechanism: enforcing orthogonality through gradient projection. In practice, these approaches compute the SVD of input representations from previous tasks, identify the dominant subspace associated with the largest singular values, and project gradients for subsequent tasks onto its orthogonal complement. In this way, updates are constrained to avoid directions that could interfere with previously learned representations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Importantly, projecting gradients onto the complement of the dominant subspace is equivalent to directly operating in the subspace spanned by the singular vectors corresponding to the smallest singular values. Both viewpoints impose the same geometric constraint. The difference lies in how the constraint is implemented: prior work enforces it during optimization via gradient projection.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">In contrast, we incorporate this constraint directly into the parameterization. Rather than modifying gradients at each step, we construct a fixed orthogonal basis from the singular vectors associated with small singular values and use it to define the allowable update space (see Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Learning in the Null Space: Small Singular Values for Continual Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>). For each layer, this basis remains fixed, and only a single trainable matrix is learned within this restricted space. As a result, the orthogonality condition is satisfied by construction, and learning proceeds directly within the approximate null space of previous inputs regardless of the selected optimizer.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">We refer to this approach as <span id="S1.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">NESS (Null-space Estimated from Small Singular values)</span>. Empirical evaluations demonstrate that this parameterization leads to stable behavior across tasks while maintaining competitive performance (see Figure <a href="#S4.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 4.1 Experimental Setup ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Learning in the Null Space: Small Singular Values for Continual Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Below is a summary of our contributions:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose <span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">NESS</span>, a continual learning (CL) algorithm that enforces orthogonality by directly parameterizing weight updates within the approximate null space of previously learned feature spaces. This constraint is implemented through a fixed subspace basis derived from small singular values, while learning a single trainable matrix per layer. We provide a theoretical analysis supporting the efficiency of this formulation.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate NESS against several orthogonality-based CL baselines on standard image classification benchmarks. The results demonstrate competitive performance and consistently improved backward transfer (i.e., reduced forgetting), indicating that NESS provides a stable and efficient approach to continual learning.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Work</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Catastrophic Forgetting in Continual Learning (CL).</span>
Numerous approaches have been proposed to address catastrophic forgetting in continual learning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>, <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>. Existing methods are commonly categorized based on their underlying mechanisms.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Memory-based methods</span> maintain a buffer of samples from previous tasks and replay them while learning new tasks; a representative example is A-GEM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>.
<span id="S2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Architecture-based methods</span> modify or expand the network structure during training to reduce interference across tasks, with early work such as HAT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>.
<span id="S2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Optimization-based methods</span> introduce additional constraints or regularization terms into the loss function to preserve prior knowledge. These methods are often divided into regularization-based approaches (e.g., EWC <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>) and subspace-based approaches <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>.
<span id="S2.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Representation-based methods</span> aim to learn task-invariant or structured representations that remain stable throughout the continual learning process.
Finally, <span id="S2.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Bayesian methods</span> formulate continual learning from a probabilistic perspective, treating catastrophic forgetting as an inference problem.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Orthogonality-based Continual Learning.</span>
Orthogonality-based approaches, which fall under optimization-based methods, constrain gradients to reduce interference across tasks. These methods can be broadly divided into two categories:</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">(i) <span id="S2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Orthogonal gradient-based methods</span>, which enforce new task gradients to be orthogonal to the gradient subspace of previous tasks. This typically requires storing or estimating gradient information from earlier tasks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">(ii) <span id="S2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Orthogonal feature-based methods</span>, which constrain gradients to be orthogonal to the subspace spanned by input features or representations of previous tasks. These approaches rely on estimating feature subspaces and generally offer improved scalability compared to gradient-storage methods.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Representative Orthogonality-based Approaches.</span>
Early works include OGD <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>, which restricts gradient directions to avoid interference, and OWM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>, which leverages a context-dependent processing module to reuse feature representations while updating the model.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Building on these ideas, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Saha et al. [8]</a></cite> proposed Gradient Projection Memory (GPM), which projects gradients onto the null space of previous task subspaces to prevent interference. Subsequent extensions improved different aspects of this framework.
FS-DGPM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite> incorporates sharpness-aware flattening to reduce the generalization gap.
TRGP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite> introduces a trust-region strategy to select important previous tasks and scales weight projections accordingly.
SGP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite> improves scalability by combining null-space projection with scaled updates along retained gradient subspaces.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p8.1" class="ltx_p">More recent developments include DFGP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>, <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>, which integrates mixup-based data augmentation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>, <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite> and sharpness-aware minimization (SAM) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite> into the gradient projection framework to improve generalization.
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al. [22]</a></cite> proposed Space Decoupling (SD), which decomposes the input space into complementary subspaces to balance stability and plasticity.
Finally, CODE-CL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite> introduces conceptor-based gradient projection, using conceptor matrices to project gradients onto pseudo-orthogonal subspaces of previous task input spaces.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p9" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p9.1" class="ltx_p">While these methods enforce orthogonality through gradient-based constraints during optimization, we next formalize the continual learning setting and introduce an alternative parameterization that incorporates the orthogonality constraint directly in the weight space.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p10" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p10.1" class="ltx_p">Recent work has also highlighted the importance of small-magnitude parameters in neural networks.
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al. [24]</a></cite> study the role of small weights and show that they can have a non-trivial impact on model behavior and generalization.
While their analysis focuses on the magnitude of weights rather than the singular value structure, it complements our perspective that low-energy components of a model or its representations may play a significant role in stability.
Investigating the relationship between small singular directions and small weights in continual learning settings is an interesting direction for future work.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Methodology</h2>

<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Problem Definition</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Continual Learning (CL) aims to train a model on a sequence of tasks while maintaining strong performance on previously learned tasks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We consider a CL setting with <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">sequential tasks</span>
<math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\mathrm{Task}_{1},\mathrm{Task}_{2},\dots,\mathrm{Task}_{T}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Task</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Task</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Task</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\mathrm{Task}_{1},\mathrm{Task}_{2},\dots,\mathrm{Task}_{T}\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
At time step <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, the model observes a dataset</p>
<table id="S3.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{t}=(X_{t},Y_{t})=\{(x_{t,i},y_{t,i})\}_{i=1}^{N_{t}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msubsup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{t}=(X_{t},Y_{t})=\{(x_{t,i},y_{t,i})\}_{i=1}^{N_{t}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the inputs and labels of task <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively, and <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of samples in <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. Each input-label pair satisfies <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{t,i}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{t,i}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{t,i}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\text{out}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>out</mtext></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{t,i}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\text{out}}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For ease of exposition, we focus on a single linear layer with parameter matrix <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times d_{\text{out}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐖</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>out</mtext></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times d_{\text{out}}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the parameters obtained after training on task <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>. Although we present the formulation for one linear layer, our method applies to an arbitrary number of linear layers in deep networks and can be implemented independently and in parallel across layers, similar in spirit to LoRA-style parameterizations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Tasks are learned sequentially, and at step <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> only data from <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is available for training.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The goal of CL is to learn parameters <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> that perform well on all tasks <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{1,\dots,T\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{1,\dots,T\}</annotation></semantics></math>. A common formulation of the objective is</p>
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}=\arg\min_{\mathbf{W}}\sum_{t=1}^{T}\mathcal{L}_{t}(\mathbf{W})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐖</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mrow><mmultiscripts><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover><mprescripts></mprescripts><mi>𝐖</mi><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐖</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}=\arg\min_{\mathbf{W}}\sum_{t=1}^{T}\mathcal{L}_{t}(\mathbf{W}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p5.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{t}(\mathbf{W})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐖</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{t}(\mathbf{W})</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the loss associated with task <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>. In practice, this joint objective cannot be optimized directly due to the sequential data constraint.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">The central difficulty in this setting is catastrophic forgetting: updating <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐖</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}</annotation></semantics></math> to minimize <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> may significantly degrade performance on previously learned tasks <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{1},\dots,\mathcal{L}_{t-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{1},\dots,\mathcal{L}_{t-1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, we mitigate catastrophic forgetting by constraining parameter updates at each training step, restricting them to subspaces that minimize interference with previously learned representations while still allowing adaptation to new tasks.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Our Proposed Method</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Following the problem definition, continual learning can be viewed as balancing stability and plasticity.
For task <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, we aim to adapt the model to new data while limiting interference with previously learned tasks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Assume the network has <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> layers. Let</p>
<table id="S3.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\mathbf{W}_{t}^{(l)}=\mathbf{W}_{t}^{(l)}-\mathbf{W}_{t-1}^{(l)}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐖</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\mathbf{W}_{t}^{(l)}=\mathbf{W}_{t}^{(l)}-\mathbf{W}_{t-1}^{(l)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">denote the weight update at layer <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math> when learning task <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, and let
<math id="S3.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}_{t}^{(l)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}_{t}^{(l)}</annotation></semantics></math>
denote the set of inputs at layer <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math> collected from tasks <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1,2,\dots,t-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1,2,\dots,t-1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Stability Constraint (Output Preservation).</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To preserve performance on earlier tasks, we require that learning task <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> does not significantly alter the outputs induced by previous inputs. This can be expressed as the per-input constraint</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\|x^{(l)\top}\Delta\mathbf{W}_{t}^{(l)}\right\|_{2}^{2}\leq\varepsilon,\quad\forall l\in[L],\;\forall x^{(l)}\in\mathcal{I}_{t}^{(l)}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em">⊤</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>≤</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\|x^{(l)\top}\Delta\mathbf{W}_{t}^{(l)}\right\|_{2}^{2}\leq\varepsilon,\quad\forall l\in[L],\;\forall x^{(l)}\in\mathcal{I}_{t}^{(l)},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math> controls the allowable output perturbation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Evaluating this constraint requires access to previous task inputs. In our implementation, such access is limited to a single forward pass per task to construct the subspace used to constrain updates. Exploring fully memory-free alternatives is left for future work.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Plasticity Objective (New Task Learning).</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Under this stability constraint, the learning problem for task <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> can be formulated conceptually as</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<table id="S3.E3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S3.E3X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E3X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\min_{\mathbf{W}_{t}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><munder><mi>min</mi><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></munder><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\min_{\mathbf{W}_{t}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E3X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{CE}(D_{t},\mathbf{W}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{CE}(D_{t},\mathbf{W}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.E3Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E3Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\text{s.t.}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext>s.t.</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{s.t.}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E3Xa.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\left\|x^{(l)\top}\Delta\mathbf{W}_{t}^{(l)}\right\|_{2}^{2}\leq\varepsilon,\quad\forall l\in[L],\;\forall x^{(l)}\in\mathcal{I}_{t}^{(l)}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mi>x</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em">⊤</mo></mrow></msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>𝐖</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>‖</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>≤</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>l</mi><mo>∈</mo><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>x</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\left\|x^{(l)\top}\Delta\mathbf{W}_{t}^{(l)}\right\|_{2}^{2}\leq\varepsilon,\quad\forall l\in[L],\;\forall x^{(l)}\in\mathcal{I}_{t}^{(l)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We emphasize that Eq. (<a href="#S3.E3" title="Equation 3 ‣ Plasticity Objective (New Task Learning). ‣ 3.2 Our Proposed Method ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Learning in the Null Space: Small Singular Values for Continual Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) serves as a conceptual formulation of the stability–plasticity trade-off. Rather than solving this constrained problem directly, we introduce a structured parameterization of <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\mathbf{W}_{t}^{(l)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\mathbf{W}_{t}^{(l)}</annotation></semantics></math> that satisfies the stability constraint by construction.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Construction of the Stability Subspace.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For clarity, we describe the construction for a single layer and omit the layer index.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let</p>
<table id="S3.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="I_{t}=[X_{1}:X_{2}:\dots:X_{t-1}]\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times\sum_{i=1}^{t-1}N_{i}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><msubsup><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em" stretchy="true">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mstyle><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{t}=[X_{1}:X_{2}:\dots:X_{t-1}]\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times\sum_{i=1}^{t-1}N_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">denote the concatenation of all previous task inputs at the considered layer.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We compute its singular value decomposition:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<table id="S3.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{t}=\tilde{U}_{t}\Sigma_{t}\tilde{V}_{t}^{\top}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>U</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{t}=\tilde{U}_{t}\Sigma_{t}\tilde{V}_{t}^{\top},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">where the singular values satisfy</p>
<table id="S3.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{t,1}\geq\sigma_{t,2}\geq\dots\geq\sigma_{t,d}\geq 0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>≥</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{t,1}\geq\sigma_{t,2}\geq\dots\geq\sigma_{t,d}\geq 0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Directions associated with small singular values correspond to directions with low energy in previously observed inputs. Restricting updates to these directions reduces interference with earlier tasks.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Selecting the Small-Singular-Value Subspace.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon_{1}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ε</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon_{1}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> be a threshold and define</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<table id="S3.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j=\min\left\{i:\sigma_{t,i}\leq\varepsilon_{1}\|I_{t}\|_{F}\right\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ε</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j=\min\left\{i:\sigma_{t,i}\leq\varepsilon_{1}\|I_{t}\|_{F}\right\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The singular vectors
<math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{u_{t,j},\dots,u_{t,d}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{u_{t,j},\dots,u_{t,d}\}</annotation></semantics></math>
span an approximate null subspace. Let <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p3.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="U_{t}=[u_{t,j}:\dots:u_{t,d}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{t}=[u_{t,j}:\dots:u_{t,d}]</annotation></semantics></math>
be the matrix formed by these columns. We parameterize the update as</p>
<table id="S3.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta W_{t}=U_{t}V_{t}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta W_{t}=U_{t}V_{t},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is fixed and only <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is trainable.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px5" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Explicit Stability Bound.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Consider any previous input <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\mathcal{I}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\mathcal{I}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Since each such <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to a column of <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists a standard basis vector <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> such that</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px5.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<table id="S3.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{\top}=e_{i}^{\top}I_{t}^{\top}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>I</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{\top}=e_{i}^{\top}I_{t}^{\top}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px5.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Using Eq. (<a href="#S3.E4" title="Equation 4 ‣ Construction of the Stability Subspace. ‣ 3.2 Our Proposed Method ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Learning in the Null Space: Small Singular Values for Continual Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>),
<math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{t}^{\top}=\tilde{V}_{t}\Sigma_{t}\tilde{U}_{t}^{\top}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>I</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>U</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{t}^{\top}=\tilde{V}_{t}\Sigma_{t}\tilde{U}_{t}^{\top}.</annotation></semantics></math>
Thus,
<math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px5.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{\top}\Delta W_{t}=e_{i}^{\top}\tilde{V}_{t}\Sigma_{t}\tilde{U}_{t}^{\top}U_{t}V_{t}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>U</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{\top}\Delta W_{t}=e_{i}^{\top}\tilde{V}_{t}\Sigma_{t}\tilde{U}_{t}^{\top}U_{t}V_{t}.</annotation></semantics></math>
Because <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px5.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> contains only singular vectors corresponding to indices <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px5.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j,\dots,d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j,\dots,d</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px5.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{U}_{t}^{\top}U_{t}=\begin{bmatrix}0\\
I\end{bmatrix}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>U</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mi>I</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{U}_{t}^{\top}U_{t}=\begin{bmatrix}0\\
I\end{bmatrix},</annotation></semantics></math>
so only the small singular values remain active. Denoting the diagonal matrix of these values by <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px5.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{\text{small}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mtext>small</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{\text{small}}</annotation></semantics></math>, we obtain
<math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px5.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{\top}\Delta W_{t}=e_{i}^{\top}\tilde{V}_{t}\Sigma_{\text{small}}V_{t}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mtext>small</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{\top}\Delta W_{t}=e_{i}^{\top}\tilde{V}_{t}\Sigma_{\text{small}}V_{t}.</annotation></semantics></math>
Taking norms,
<math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px5.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|x^{\top}\Delta W_{t}\|_{2}\leq\|\Sigma_{\text{small}}\|_{2}\|V_{t}\|_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mtext>small</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|x^{\top}\Delta W_{t}\|_{2}\leq\|\Sigma_{\text{small}}\|_{2}\|V_{t}\|_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>
(notice that <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px5.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{V}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{V}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is orthogonal), with
<math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px5.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\Sigma_{\text{small}}\|_{2}\leq\varepsilon_{1}\|I_{t}\|_{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mtext>small</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ε</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\Sigma_{\text{small}}\|_{2}\leq\varepsilon_{1}\|I_{t}\|_{F}</annotation></semantics></math>
(by construction). Therefore, for every previous input <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px5.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="S3.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|x^{\top}\Delta W_{t}\|_{2}\leq\varepsilon_{1}\|I_{t}\|_{F}\|V_{t}\|_{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ε</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|x^{\top}\Delta W_{t}\|_{2}\leq\varepsilon_{1}\|I_{t}\|_{F}\|V_{t}\|_{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px5.p3.2" class="ltx_p">If we enforce</p>
<table id="S3.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|V_{t}\|_{2}\leq\frac{\sqrt{\varepsilon}}{\varepsilon_{1}\|I_{t}\|_{F}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><mfrac><msqrt><mi>ε</mi></msqrt><mrow><msub><mi>ε</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|V_{t}\|_{2}\leq\frac{\sqrt{\varepsilon}}{\varepsilon_{1}\|I_{t}\|_{F}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px5.p3.3" class="ltx_p">then
<math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px5.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|x^{\top}\Delta W_{t}\|_{2}^{2}\leq\varepsilon,\quad\forall x\in\mathcal{I}_{t}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>≤</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|x^{\top}\Delta W_{t}\|_{2}^{2}\leq\varepsilon,\quad\forall x\in\mathcal{I}_{t}.</annotation></semantics></math>
Hence, the stability constraint in Eq. (<a href="#S3.E2" title="Equation 2 ‣ Stability Constraint (Output Preservation). ‣ 3.2 Our Proposed Method ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Learning in the Null Space: Small Singular Values for Continual Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>)
is satisfied for every previous input.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px6" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Practical Enforcement.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In practice, the bound in Eq. (<a href="#S3.E8" title="Equation 8 ‣ Explicit Stability Bound. ‣ 3.2 Our Proposed Method ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Learning in the Null Space: Small Singular Values for Continual Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>) can be enforced via standard weight decay on <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> during training.
Thus, by construction, updates lie in an approximate null subspace of previous inputs, while their magnitudes remain controlled, ensuring bounded interference across tasks.
The complete NESS training procedure is summarized in Algorithm <a href="#algorithm1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ Practical Enforcement. ‣ 3.2 Our Proposed Method ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Learning in the Null Space: Small Singular Values for Continual Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="algorithm1" class="ltx_float ltx_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="algorithm1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span>NESS</figcaption>
<div id="algorithm1.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_lst_numbers_left ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="algorithm1.4.1" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Input :</span> <math id="algorithm1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X=[x_{i}]\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=[x_{i}]\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times N}</annotation></semantics></math> (input matrix with <math id="algorithm1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> samples), <math id="algorithm1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ε</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> (threshold), <math id="algorithm1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\text{out}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>out</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\text{out}}</annotation></semantics></math> (output dimension)
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.2" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Output :</span> <math id="algorithm1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> (frozen orthogonal bases), <math id="algorithm1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> (trainable matrix)
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.3" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">1</span></span>
              <span id="algorithm1.4.3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">function</span> <em id="algorithm1.4.3.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_typewriter">GetUV(<em id="algorithm1.4.3.3.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_serif ltx_font_italic"><math id="algorithm1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X,\varepsilon_{1},d_{\text{out}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ε</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">out</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X,\varepsilon_{1},d_{\text{out}}</annotation></semantics></math></em>)</em>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.4" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> 
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">2</span></span>
              <math id="algorithm1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\leftarrow XX^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\leftarrow XX^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math>   <span id="algorithm1.4.4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">// </span><span id="algorithm1.4.4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">covariance matrix</span>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.5" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> 
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">3</span></span>
              <math id="algorithm1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{U},\mathbf{\Lambda}\leftarrow\text{eig}(C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐔</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>𝚲</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mtext>eig</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{U},\mathbf{\Lambda}\leftarrow\text{eig}(C)</annotation></semantics></math> 
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.6" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> 
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">4</span></span>
              <span id="algorithm1.4.6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">// </span><span id="algorithm1.4.6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Eigenvalues are sorted in descending order </span>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.7" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> 
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">5</span></span>
              <math id="algorithm1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\sigma_{1},\dots,\sigma_{d}\}\leftarrow\sqrt{\text{diag}(\mathbf{\Lambda})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><msqrt><mrow><mtext>diag</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝚲</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\sigma_{1},\dots,\sigma_{d}\}\leftarrow\sqrt{\text{diag}(\mathbf{\Lambda})}</annotation></semantics></math> 
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.8" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> 
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">6</span></span>
              <math id="algorithm1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{u_{1},\dots,u_{d}\}\leftarrow\text{Column}(\mathbf{U})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mtext>Column</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐔</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{u_{1},\dots,u_{d}\}\leftarrow\text{Column}(\mathbf{U})</annotation></semantics></math> 
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.9" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> 
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.9.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">7</span></span>
              <math id="algorithm1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j=\min\{k\;:\;\sigma_{k}\leq\varepsilon_{1}\|X\|_{F}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ε</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j=\min\{k\;:\;\sigma_{k}\leq\varepsilon_{1}\|X\|_{F}\}</annotation></semantics></math>  <span id="algorithm1.4.9.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#808080;">// select small-singular-value directions</span>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.10" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> 
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.10.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">8</span></span>
              <math id="algorithm1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{t}\leftarrow[u_{j},\dots,u_{d}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{t}\leftarrow[u_{j},\dots,u_{d}]</annotation></semantics></math>  <span id="algorithm1.4.10.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#808080;">// <math id="algorithm1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times(d-j+1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#808080;" mathcolor="#808080">U</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#808080;" mathcolor="#808080">t</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#808080;" mathcolor="#808080">∈</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#808080;" mathcolor="#808080">ℝ</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#808080;" mathcolor="#808080">d</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#808080;" lspace="0.222em" mathcolor="#808080" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#808080;" mathcolor="#808080" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#808080;" mathcolor="#808080">d</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#808080;" mathcolor="#808080">−</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#808080;" mathcolor="#808080">j</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#808080;" mathcolor="#808080">+</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#808080;" mathcolor="#808080">1</mn></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#808080;" mathcolor="#808080" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times(d-j+1)}</annotation></semantics></math></span>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.11" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> 
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.11.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">9</span></span>
              <math id="algorithm1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{t}\leftarrow\mathbf{0}\in\mathbb{R}^{(d-j+1)\times d_{\text{out}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mn>𝟎</mn><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>out</mtext></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{t}\leftarrow\mathbf{0}\in\mathbb{R}^{(d-j+1)\times d_{\text{out}}}</annotation></semantics></math> 
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.12" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> 
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.12.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">10</span></span>
              <span id="algorithm1.4.12.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">return</span> <math id="algorithm1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{t},V_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{t},V_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.13" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.13.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Input :</span> <math id="algorithm1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> (current task index), <math id="algorithm1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{t}=(X_{t},Y_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{t}=(X_{t},Y_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> (task dataset), <math id="algorithm1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ε</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> (threshold), 
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.14" class="ltx_listingline"><math id="algorithm1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}=[1,2,\dots,L]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}=[1,2,\dots,L]</annotation></semantics></math> (layers), <math id="algorithm1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{W}=[W^{(l)}]_{l=1}^{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>L</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{W}=[W^{(l)}]_{l=1}^{L}</annotation></semantics></math> (current weights)
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.15" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.15.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Output :</span> Updated weights <math id="algorithm1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{W}</annotation></semantics></math>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.16" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.16.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">11</span></span>
              <span id="algorithm1.4.16.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">function</span> <em id="algorithm1.4.16.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_typewriter">train(<em id="algorithm1.4.16.3.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_serif ltx_font_italic"><math id="algorithm1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t,D_{t},\varepsilon_{1},\mathcal{L},\mathcal{W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ε</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t,D_{t},\varepsilon_{1},\mathcal{L},\mathcal{W}</annotation></semantics></math></em>)</em>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.17" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> 
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.17.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">12</span></span>
              <span id="algorithm1.4.17.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <em id="algorithm1.4.17.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">each layer <math id="algorithm1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l\in\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l\in\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math></em> <span id="algorithm1.4.17.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.18" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>  <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> 
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.18.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">13</span></span>
              <math id="algorithm1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I^{(l)}\leftarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>I</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I^{(l)}\leftarrow</annotation></semantics></math> forward pass to collect inputs of layer <math id="algorithm1.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="algorithm1.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> 
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.19" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>  <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> 
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.19.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">14</span></span>
              <math id="algorithm1.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(U^{(l)},V^{(l)})\leftarrow\texttt{GetUV}(I^{(l)},\varepsilon_{1},\text{output\_dim of layer }l)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>U</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">GetUV</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>I</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ε</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mtext>output_dim of layer </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(U^{(l)},V^{(l)})\leftarrow\texttt{GetUV}(I^{(l)},\varepsilon_{1},\text{output\_dim of layer }l)</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.20" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> 
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.20.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">15</span></span>
              <span id="algorithm1.4.20.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">// </span><span id="algorithm1.4.20.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Train only <math id="algorithm1.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{(l)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{(l)}</annotation></semantics></math> (backbone frozen); enforce <math id="algorithm1.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|V^{(l)}\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|V^{(l)}\|</annotation></semantics></math> bound via regularization </span>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.21" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> 
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.21.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">16</span></span>
              <math id="algorithm1.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{V^{(l)}\}_{l=1}^{L}\leftarrow\arg\min_{\{V\}}\mathcal{L}_{CE}\!\left(D_{t},\;\{W^{(l)}+U^{(l)}V^{(l)}\}_{l=1}^{L}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>L</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>min</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msup><mi>W</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>U</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>L</mi></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{V^{(l)}\}_{l=1}^{L}\leftarrow\arg\min_{\{V\}}\mathcal{L}_{CE}\!\left(D_{t},\;\{W^{(l)}+U^{(l)}V^{(l)}\}_{l=1}^{L}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.22" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> 
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.22.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">17</span></span>
              <span id="algorithm1.4.22.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <em id="algorithm1.4.22.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">each layer <math id="algorithm1.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l\in\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l\in\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math></em> <span id="algorithm1.4.22.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.23" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>  <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> 
