</section>
<section id="A1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.2 </span>Additional Results</h3>

<div id="A1.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">The Value of Threshold <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ε</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. </span>
For our experiments, we have used different values for the threshold <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ε</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, ranging from 0.01 to 0.0001. Through our experiments on three different datasets, we observed that for every optimizer, <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon_{1}=0.001" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ε</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.001</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon_{1}=0.001</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon_{1}=0.0005" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ε</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.0005</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon_{1}=0.0005</annotation></semantics></math> achieved better results.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Additional experiments. </span>
We also conducted an additional experiment on DFGP without using mixup and data augmentation as another baseline for NESS. The results show that without mixup and augmentation technique, the performance of DFGP can still compare with the original DFGP. However, it still can not achieve the BWT rate of NESS.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Detail results. </span>
The detailed results of every experiment are shown as 2 types: (i) <span id="A1.SS2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">detailed results</span> (CIFAR-100 in Table <a href="#A1.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ A.2 Additional Results ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Learning in the Null Space: Small Singular Values for Continual Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, 5-datasets in Table <a href="#A1.T7" title="Table 7 ‣ A.2 Additional Results ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Learning in the Null Space: Small Singular Values for Continual Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>, MiniImageNet in Table <a href="#A1.T9" title="Table 9 ‣ A.2 Additional Results ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Learning in the Null Space: Small Singular Values for Continual Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>); and (ii) <span id="A1.SS2.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">average performance</span> (CIFAR-100 in Table <a href="#A1.T6" title="Table 6 ‣ A.2 Additional Results ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Learning in the Null Space: Small Singular Values for Continual Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, 5-datasets in Table <a href="#A1.T8" title="Table 8 ‣ A.2 Additional Results ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Learning in the Null Space: Small Singular Values for Continual Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>, MiniImageNet in Table <a href="#A1.T10" title="Table 10 ‣ A.2 Additional Results ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Learning in the Null Space: Small Singular Values for Continual Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>). For each experiment, we chose 5 different seeds: 1 (following previous works on CIFAR-100), 2, 3, 4, and 37 (following previous works on 5-datasets and MiniImageNet).</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Additional figures. </span>
Additional visualization for our method compared to the baselines is in Figure <a href="#A1.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ A.2 Additional Results ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Learning in the Null Space: Small Singular Values for Continual Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, Figure <a href="#A1.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ A.2 Additional Results ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Learning in the Null Space: Small Singular Values for Continual Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> (for CIFAR-100 experiment), and in Figure <a href="#A1.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ A.2 Additional Results ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Learning in the Null Space: Small Singular Values for Continual Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> (for MiniImageNet experiment).</p>
</div>
